;; Declare the procedures you write with define-cps-procedure rather than
;; define.  It behaves the same, and also keeps enough of the code for the tail
;; position test, which looks for calls to count-sublists-cps and apply-k from a
;; non-tail position.  That test looks procedures up by identity, so a plain
;; define would not be found, and tracing rebinds a name to a wrapper -- leave
