;; Define the macro vector-let.  It looks like this:
;;
;; (vector-let vec-expr ((name1 index1) (name2 index2) ...) body)
;;
;; vec-expr is an expression that evaluates to a vector.  Each
;; (name index) pair binds name to the element of that vector
;; at position index (using 0-based indexing, as in vector-ref).
;; All of the bound names are visible in body.
;;
;; Reminder: use vector-ref to read an element of a vector:
;;
;; (vector-ref vec index)
;;
;; For example, (vector-ref #(10 20 30) 1) evaluates to 20.
;;
;; For example:
;;
;; (vector-let #(10 20 30) ((a 0) (b 2)) (+ a b))
;;
;; evaluates to 40.
;;
;; The index expressions can be arbitrary expressions, including
;; variables bound by an enclosing let:
;;
;; (let ((i 1) (j 3))
;;   (vector-let #(10 20 30 40) ((a i) (b j)) (- b a)))
;;
;; evaluates to 20.
;;
;; The vector expression can itself be a variable bound by an
;; enclosing let:
;;
;; (let ((v #(10 20 30)))
;;   (vector-let v ((a 0) (b 2)) (+ a b)))
;;
;; evaluates to 40.
;;
;; An empty set of bindings is allowed:
;;
;; (vector-let #(1 2 3) () 42)
;;
;; evaluates to 42.
;;
;; This question is worth 20 points.
;;
;; For full credit, ensure that vec-expr is evaluated only once, no
;; matter how many names are bound.  A solution that evaluates vec-expr
;; once per binding works but will receive a small overall point
;; deduction.

;; NOTE: I have included the original starting code here in the comments:
;;
;; (define-syntax (vector-let stx)
;;  (syntax-case stx ()
;;   [(vector-let _ ...) ; not a case you'd actually want, just a placeholder so the tests run
;;     #'(nyi)]))
;;
;; Broken macros have a tendency to prevent the whole file from working.  This makes grading hard.
;; If you can't solve this problem and all tests are broken please leave your solution commented 
;; out and leave the starting code in so that the rest of the automated tests can run on your submission.

(define-syntax (vector-let stx)
  (syntax-case stx ()
    [(vector-let _ ...) ; not a case you'd actually want, just a placeholder so the tests run
     #'(nyi)]))
    


